>
>So having read spoilers and whatnot, and STILL being pretty clueless on
>how to use pets properly, I turn to you, the RGRN elite. Are there any
>pearls of wisdom on using pets that I should really know to use them
>effectively, so I can play better as characters that struggle in the
>beginning (e.g., Wizards)?
>

  - If your pet won't stay with you, you've got to stay with your pet.
    (Think of it as working with the usual lunkheads in an AD&D party.)
  - Pets are more likely to move toward you if they are 2 or fewer 
    squares away and they can see you.  Learning to lead your pet has
    many other applications in the game:  Leading heavy sacrifodder
    onto altars, wraith leading, leading nurses to a safe interaction 
    area, leading hostiles into beartraps/pits, etc.
  - Non-cursed leashes are great for pet-herding.  Carrying a tripe 
    ration in open inventory is even better.  Magic whistles are 
    perfect.
  - Learn to displace your pet into corridors, then herd it forward.
  - Shop robbing is an art in and of itself with pets.
  - The extra food in Sokoban can make it easier to deal with pets' 
    food consumption.  

  - Pets only step reluctantly on a square with a cursed item on it 
    (Unless there is food there, whereupon Ol' Greediguts will 
    cheerfully leap upon a cursed -4 studded leather armor with no 
    warning.)
    - This lets you identify non-cursed items.  Pet buc identifying
      is just as important as their role as self-targeting ranged 
      weapons.
    - This can block a pet's path.  This can be both useful (in, say, 
      Sokoban) and/or annoying, depending.

  - Build up your pet.  Let it get the small stuff for experience with 
    minimal danger.  Two fully developed large dogs/cats can clear out 
    the Minetown Guard.  Some players leave their pets unnamed so the 
    game descriptions say how big they are.  This is not perfect as each
    of the three stages of a pet's development have sublevels of 
    development.
  - Watch where your pet moves, then follow its path.  Think of your pet
    as a voluntary self-mobile trap detector.

  - Is your pet about to starve to death?  Leave the level until it goes
    peaceful/feral from hunger.  Then retame as desired.  
  - Hostile, but tameable, domestic animals, i.e. cats, dogs and horses,
    can be made peaceful or tamed by throwing food at them.  
    Inappropriate food (e.g. lichen corpse for a dog, or a tin for any 
    domestic) will make the animal peaceful.  
  - Horses must be tamed with vegetarian food, but are relatively simple
    to make peaceful.  I believe horses can be tamed with a food ration.
    Tame horses will not eat a food ration unless starving.
  - Fortune cookies are the best pet-tamers.  Almost no nutrition so 
    much more efficient than a food ration or corpse.  (They might
    even work on horses.)
  - Horses eventually develop into warhorses, which will attack even 
    priests and shopkeepers, and probably lose.  A warhorse can handle
    just about anything you'll meet in the Gnomish Mines.

  - Don't be afraid to have up to three pets at once.  
    - You'll probably end up losing at least two early on.  (Polymorph, 
      trapdoors, teleport, death, etc.)
    - A couple of large pets will let you get on with exploring while 
      they play with their food.  Then they'll be useful for identifying
      all the non-cursed loot.
    - Killer bees?  Put down some dust Elbereths and watch the fun.  

  Sokoban 
    - Great for the extra food to deal with your pets food consumption.
    - Possibility of a tripe ration!  (aka Pet Velcro)  Be sure you 
      explore each level first to keep your pets from eating any such.
    - Pets on the level with you are both good and bad.  They can stop
      hostiles from bothering you, but they'll get in the way 
      themselves.


